    "type": "archive Q&A",
    "topic": "stack exchange dump",
    "problem": "I'm new to C# and am trying to wrap my mind around object oriented programming. Here's what I am trying to accomplish: I've got a Windows Form application with Form1 and a separate class called connect (same namespace). I understand that it is good practice to use backgroundworkers when executing lengthy amounts of code (so as not to freeze the UI). So I've created a background worker in Form1\u2026",
    "solution": "You could break your steps of the connect class into separate methods and invoke them sequentially as below.You dont have to trigger the progresschanged event from connect class object. ``` private void backgroundWorker1_DoWork(object sender, DoWorkEventArgs e) { ConnectClass conObject= new ConnectClass(); backgroundWorker.ReportProgress(30, \"Connecting ...\"); conObject.Connect();\u2026",
    "source": "stackexchange"
  }
